WEBVTT

legitimacy-04-1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:06.391
Previously: to have an “us,” a line must
first be drawn; on the other side, there

legitimacy-04-2
00:00:06.391 --> 00:00:07.900
must always be a “them.”

legitimacy-04-3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:14.450
This time: five forces are loosening the
whole production line.

legitimacy-04-4
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:20.980
Episode 4: Five Forces That Loosen the
Line

legitimacy-04-5
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.010
Transnational identities

legitimacy-04-6
00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:30.501
Suppose a child holds two passports. When
the two countries meet at the World Cup,

legitimacy-04-7
00:00:30.501 --> 00:00:34.050
she holds two little flags, unsure whom
to cheer for.

legitimacy-04-8
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.816
“Which country are you from?” no longer
has just one answer. The boundaries of

legitimacy-04-9
00:00:38.816 --> 00:00:40.160
“us” begin to overlap.

legitimacy-04-10
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.066
Author's thesis §3.1: dual nationality,
diasporic identification, and

legitimacy-04-11
00:00:42.066 --> 00:00:44.193
transnational solidarity weaken the
exclusivity of national belonging (citing

legitimacy-04-12
00:00:44.193 --> 00:00:45.850
Vertovec, 2009; Levitt &amp; Glick Schiller,
2004). (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-04-13
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.300
Technological deterritorialization

legitimacy-04-14
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.911
She lives here, but at three in the
morning she meets with a company in

legitimacy-04-15
00:00:54.911 --> 00:00:59.846
another country. A platform pays her
wages and sets the rules; her data sits

legitimacy-04-16
00:00:59.846 --> 00:01:01.210
on servers elsewhere.

legitimacy-04-17
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:06.266
State borders remain, but more and more
of her daily life is governed by actors

legitimacy-04-18
00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:07.610
other than the state.

legitimacy-04-19
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.459
Author's thesis §3.1: digital
technologies let flows of capital,

legitimacy-04-20
00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:11.538
information, and power bypass states;
platform protocols govern “digital

legitimacy-04-21
00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:13.300
subjects” (citing Castells, 1996; Sassen,
2006). (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-04-22
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.540
Symbolic fragmentation

legitimacy-04-23
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:24.224
Same country, same day. One person sees a
solemn flag raising, another a satirical

legitimacy-04-24
00:01:24.224 --> 00:01:27.240
joke, and a third an angry long post.

legitimacy-04-25
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.794
People no longer share the same story.
The grand narrative of “us” begins to

legitimacy-04-26
00:01:32.794 --> 00:01:34.110
lose their belief.

legitimacy-04-27
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.856
Author's thesis §3.1: Lyotard's (1984)
“incredulity toward metanarratives”;

legitimacy-04-28
00:01:36.856 --> 00:01:39.675
Butler's (1990) identity performances can
fail, be parodied, and be given new

legitimacy-04-29
00:01:39.675 --> 00:01:40.480
meanings. (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-04-30
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.510
Risk governance

legitimacy-04-31
00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:49.642
During the pandemic years, you scanned a
code before entering. The gate asked not

legitimacy-04-32
00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:53.380
whether you were “one of us,” but what
color your risk was.

legitimacy-04-33
00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:59.718
What is managed is no longer a member of
a nation, but a set of risk data to be

legitimacy-04-34
00:01:59.718 --> 00:02:00.600
calculated.

legitimacy-04-35
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.805
Author's thesis §3.1: late-modern
governance revolves around risks within

legitimacy-04-36
00:02:02.805 --> 00:02:05.191
systems (citing Beck, 1992); populations
are governed as biological-statistical

legitimacy-04-37
00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.550
objects (citing Foucault, 2008).
(Paraphrase)

legitimacy-04-38
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.580
The disappearance of the Other

legitimacy-04-39
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:15.941
The Cold War ends. The once unmistakable
“enemy” poster on the wall fades. The

legitimacy-04-40
00:02:15.941 --> 00:02:20.820
external “them” grows less clear, while
the internal “them” multiplies.

legitimacy-04-41
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:26.989
“We must exist because they threaten
us”—that sentence can no longer find its

legitimacy-04-42
00:02:26.989 --> 00:02:27.550
object.

legitimacy-04-43
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.904
Author's thesis §3.1: internal Others
multiply; the external Other becomes

legitimacy-04-44
00:02:29.904 --> 00:02:32.290
internal and hard to identify. The
fear-based mechanism of legitimacy fails

legitimacy-04-45
00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:33.340
(citing Said, 1978). (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-04-46
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:43.170
Count them: how many of these five
situations have you experienced?

legitimacy-04-47
00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.701
To be clear: coming loose is not breaking
apart. Passports are still issued, gates

legitimacy-04-48
00:02:49.701 --> 00:02:52.380
still open, and taxes are still
collected.

legitimacy-04-49
00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.497
Something else changes: the machine still
runs, but no longer carries meaning of

legitimacy-04-50
00:02:57.497 --> 00:03:01.911
its own. The state remains as an
institution; as an answer to “who we

legitimacy-04-51
00:03:01.911 --> 00:03:03.510
are,” it starts to drift.

legitimacy-04-52
00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.588
Author's thesis §3.1: the state still
exists institutionally, but loses its

legitimacy-04-53
00:03:06.588 --> 00:03:08.230
symbolic anchor and floats. (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-04-54
00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.690
The passport is still there.

legitimacy-04-55
00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:17.740
Its edges are just beginning to blur.

legitimacy-04-56
00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.680
This episode's reference card (take a
screenshot)

legitimacy-04-57
00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.100
Transnational identities | Two passports,
two little flags | The boundaries of “us”

legitimacy-04-58
